Keep the built-in server's data store alive across stop/start cycles

The in-app Modbus server lost every register, coil and input value on
each stop/start: ConnectionManager's transport match did not recognize
a ModbusServerService as fitting its server-mode profile, so every
reconnect disposed the running server and created a fresh one, and
ModbusServerService.DisconnectAsync disposed the dispatcher even when
the service was only being stopped.

- ConnectionManager: match the service kind against the profile (server
  mode + TCP -> ModbusServerService), so reconnects reuse the instance
- ModbusServerService: DisconnectAsync now only stops the dispatcher;
  ConnectAsync reuses it (it owns the per-unit DataStores); only the
  service's Dispose destroys it (DisposeAsyncCore still disposes via
  the new cleanup path)
- ConnectionManager: RemoveProfile takes the service out of the table
  first and tears it down in order (disconnect -> dispose -> event)
  instead of racing a fire-and-forget disconnect against an immediate
  Dispose; a stale saved ActiveProfileId now falls back to the first
  loaded profile
- ModbusTcpService: a reconnect disposes the previous master/socket
  under the I/O lock instead of leaking them

Adds regression tests: server reconnect keeps the service instance and
the written register value; stale ActiveProfileId fallback; removing a
connected server completes the teardown and actually frees the port.
